Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase or sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein that generates membrane potential in animal cells, as it moves 3Na⁺ out, while moving 2K⁺ in, generating an overall negative charge in the cell.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for the movement of molecules across the cell membrane.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cells are typically negatively charged\">Cells are typically negatively charged</a><ul><li>The pump mentioned herein is what keeps the cell negatively charged.</li></ul></li></ul>","All philosophy reduces to metaphysics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that any philosophical debate is fundamentally a metaphysical debate.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this were the case, then we could essentially simplify any debate down to the level of metaphysics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For instance, debates such as the nature of morality can often be regarded merely as debates about what is counts as a “thing” or the relatively ontological statuses of things which make them ethically different. Even claims in metaphysics such as “what is art” seems to be fundamentally a question of ontology, rather than a question of what “aesthetically” (whatever that means) counts as art. In logic, different logical systems (classical, modal), seem all to have different metaphysics assumptions which is what fundamentally makes them different. A weaker version of this claim, which seems to be better generally supported is the idea that all philosophy makes metaphysical assumptions, and therefore, it is the fundamental branch of philosophy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>This is another example?</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The very nature of Chinese philosophy, and the circumstances under which it emerged dictates that it is inseparable from political theory.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial feature of Chinese philosophy that sets it apart from Western philosophy. It also again shows the pragmatic nature that fundamentally underlies Chinese philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is often a direct link between various Chinese philosophies and the specific politics that they endorse. For instance, Confucianism advocates for a strict social and political hierarchy, while Taoism strives for a more anarcho-primitivist outlook.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a><ul><li>This seems related, since the state can be a crucial instrument in aiding the individual achieve spiritual elevation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>Again, shows the pragmatic nature of Chinese thought.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>Direct example of Confucianism influence Chinese state politics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Essays (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A deeply personal, wide-ranging exploration of the human condition, mixing philosophy, anecdote, self-examination, and reflection on everyday life. Written in the late 16th century, they are notable for their skeptical, curious, and tolerant outlook: Montaigne questions certainty, examines his own thoughts and habits, and argues that understanding oneself is a path to understanding humanity more broadly. Rather than presenting a fixed system, the <em>Essays</em> invite readers into a flexible, wandering form of thinking that values experience, moderation, and honest observation over dogma.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>There are many impactful messages scattered throughout this book, making it one of my most interesting reads.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We have power over nothing except our will\">We have power over nothing except our will</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has to confront their problems head on\">One has to confront their problems head on</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Free will is impossible, regardless of if we accept determinism or not, since either way, we are not able to self-determine.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this argument succeeds, the debate over compatibilism is rendered null, and free will is determined to be impossible.</p><h4>Argument</h4><p>The Basic Argument goes as follows:</p><ol><li>You do what you do because of the way you are.<ul><li>Your actions can be thought of as a function $\\alpha$ which takes many states, i.e., the way that you mentally are, your location, your height, etc. into account, which together determine your actions. $$</li></ul></li></ol><p>\\alpha(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}, \\dots, x_{n}) = Your \\ Actions $$</p><ol><li>To be truly responsible for what you do, you must be responsible for the way you are. You also must explicitly choose the way that you are.<ul><li>In other words, you must be able to determine $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$, etc. But this requires some prior state, $P_{1}$, which regards your attitudes towards a certain range of subjects relevant for the states of $\\alpha$.</li></ul></li><li>You are the way you are because of circumstances outside your control.<ul><li>However, for $P_{1}$ to actually determine anything, it must also be the case that you choose $P_{1}$, based on some other set of principles, $P_{2}$.</li><li>This results in infinite regress.</li></ul></li><li>Therefore, you are not responsible for your nature, and thus not responsible for your actions.<ul><li>True moral responsibility is impossible, since it requires true self-determination, which is impossible.</li></ul></li></ol><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The Basic Argument is not to say that you cannot change yourself, but that you cannot change yourself in a way that matters for moral responsibility.</p><ul><li>In the moment, it seems that we always have the freedom of choice and that we have moral responsibility.</li></ul><p>It seems true to say that, in general, we believe that moral responsibility for what one does is somehow involved with responsibility for the way that one is.</p><ul><li>An understanding of moral responsibility comes from an understanding of one’s ability to true shape oneself.</li></ul><h5>Possible Responses</h5><ol><li><em>Compatibilism</em>: rejecting premise (2) of the argument by saying that you can be morally responsible for what you do even if you do not choose the way that you are intentionally, i.e., even if determinism is true.<ul><li>It seems that this does not really dispel the central moral intuition that we are only responsible for something if we ourselves are self-determined.</li></ul></li><li><em>Libertarianism</em>: argues that determinism is false, and therefore, we have moral responsibility.<ul><li>But it seems that this does not fundamentally solve the issue, since if determinism is false, then indeterminacy must be true. And it seems that indeterminacy does not rescue moral responsibility.</li></ul></li></ol><p>We could also attempt to introduced a factor $S$ that exists beyond our “determined” factors ($CPM$, or character, personality, mental state, etc.). However, how this $S$ is formed is again called into question.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism\">The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism</a><ul><li>Closes off the libertarian escape route specifically, where the Basic Argument closes off both.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>This note makes the two notes above irrelevant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities\">The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities</a><ul><li>Another critique of free will.</li></ul></li></ul>","In life, one has nothing to regret":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is nothing for us to regret in life, since even if we had acted differently, things would probably have turned out the same way. In other words, in most circumstances, things are simply not as important as it might be suggested in the moment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that, once understood, changes the way that we look at life. A lot of things that previously seemed like a big deal suddenly become a lot more manageable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The direction of ones life is not easily changed, and by struggling against one, one will never be able to “conquer life.” Rather, a more sensible strategy is to accept the ebbs and flows that life presents us, and remember that even though things might seem earth-shattering at the moment, in the long run, they are not that influential. A reason for this is that our life is built up through small moments, a constant accumulation. Our life is more akin to Darwinian evolution than Lamarkianism. We change slowly, and there are very few moments that actually decide our life directions, even though it may seem that way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>It seems that this is also something implied by the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Life is too short for regrets\">Life is too short for regrets</a><ul><li>Something else implied by this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the undiversified investor, since the market price does not actually account for the firm level risk of a firm (since institutional investors usually hold diversified portfolios), the market price is usually overvalued for the undiversified investor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why an investor should not be undiversified.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the investor looking to invest in a specific company, the company’s price will almost always be above the rational amount that they would be willing to pay since the institutional investor is willing to pay a higher price since they do not account for the firm risk (which is balanced out through their diversified portfolio).</p><h4>References</h4>","Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that intellectualism and rationalism can only be used to rationalize selfishness. The only way for love to occur is through impulsiveness and emotion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important ideas from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>TBK</em>, Ivan continues to rationalize, and he does so to such a degree that he is genuinely unable to love.</p><ul><li>For instance, when he comes upon the freezing peasant the second time, in an impulsive decision, he carries the man to the police station and hopes for his recovery.</li><li>He also originally at the desire to confess about his role in the murder of his father, and Dimitri’s innocence, but he rationalized this action away, saying that he will do it tomorrow (after the trial starts).</li><li>However, when he does go in tomorrow, his account doesn’t make anymore sense, since if he were truly responsible, he would have confessed to it earlier.</li></ul><p>Dimitri’s impulsiveness and baseness is eventually what allows him to love others.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular\">It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular</a><ul><li>Whereas the former love is an intellectual love, the latter love is “true love,” in the sense that it is joining humanity’s shared connection.</li></ul></li></ul>","Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Unlike the West, which places its emphasis on loyalty to the nation-state, Islamic cultures have a much heavier emphasis on loyalty on the clan level, and beyond the nation-state level, regarding the <em>ummah</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the lack of inter-state conflict in the Islamic world. This also means that the West’s focus on the nation-state level may be misguided.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Western civilizations, when viewing their loyalty, can be seen to place the largest emphasis to loyalty on a specific nation-state, not one’s immediate community or the large religious community that one is part of. Islamic civilizations on the other hand, emphasize exactly the opposite. Of note is that while the <em>ummah</em> explicitly presupposes the illegitimacy of the nation-state, it would only be able to be united through the actions of such a state. Otherwise, there is no political unity.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Perhaps it is this ignorance of the supremacy of the nation-state that allowed Islam to be spread to effectively through trade?</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice as fairness fosters self-respect since the public institutions affirms the sense that one’s rational, long-term plan in life is worth carrying out. Also, this leads us to respect others, which in turn means others respect us. This eventually leads to more societal harmony and efficient social cooperation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a part of Rawls’ argument for justice as fairness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions\">Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions</a><ul><li>This explains why we deal with institutions, and thus its institutions which support our plans.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Self-respect is the most important primary good\">Self-respect is the most important primary good</a><ul><li>This is why it matters that Justice as Fairness promotes self-respect.</li></ul></li></ul>","Nicomachean Ethics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A foundational work of ethics that introduced the theory of Virtue Ethics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has had a tremendous impact on the way that future ethicists approach their discipline.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><h5>The Good and Happiness</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good\">Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative\">The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative</a></li></ul><h5>Moral Virtue and the Mean</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Moral States</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated\">Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do\">The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Acting virtuously is not painful\">Acting virtuously is not painful</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself\">Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal\">The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a></li></ul><h5>Voluntariness, Choice, and Deliberation</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards\">An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance\">An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily\">Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end\">A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a></li></ul><h5>The Particular Virtues</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be courageous is not to be afraid of death\">To be courageous is not to be afraid of death</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Endurance is a crucial component of courage\">Endurance is a crucial component of courage</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself\">Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions\">Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnanimity is the &#x27;crown&#x27; of the moral virtues\">Magnanimity is the 'crown' of the moral virtues</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling\">Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling</a></li></ul><h5>Justice</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Justice</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals\">Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction\">There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Equity is the rectification of legal justice\">Equity is the rectification of legal justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices\">To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly\">One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a></li></ul><h5>Intellectual Virtue and Prudence</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself\">The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence\">Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action\">The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a></li></ul><h5>Continence and Incontinence</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Obstinacy is different from continence\">Obstinacy is different from continence</a></li></ul><h5>Friendship</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality\">A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodwill is not friendship\">Goodwill is not friendship</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state\">Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household\">Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around\">The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our best friend is ourselves\">Our best friend is ourselves</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtuous man needs friends\">The virtuous man needs friends</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity\">Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with\">We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with</a></li></ul><h5>Pleasure and Contemplation</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle on Pleasure</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not a process\">Pleasure is not a process</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad\">Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable\">What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness\">Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>The continuation of the argument: the Ethics establishes what makes an individual good, and the Politics asks what kind of city produces such individuals.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Desert and the Basic Structure</span><ul><li>The map that sets the account of distributive justice given here against the Rawlsian one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism (book)\">Utilitarianism (book)</a><ul><li>The main rival account of the human good, and the one against which virtue ethics is usually defined.</li></ul></li></ul>","On Liberty":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><p>Taken together the three are the core of nineteenth-century liberalism, and reading them as one argument rather than three books is what makes Mill's position intelligible: the political conclusions in the second and third works are supposed to follow from the moral theory in the first.</p><h5>I. Introductory</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals\">We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals</a><ul><li>The harm principle, which the rest of the essay is an elaboration of.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a></li></ul><h5>II. Of the Liberty of Thought and Discussion</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality\">Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth\">Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism\">Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism</a><ul><li>The objection Mill answers, not his own position.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a></li></ul><h5>III. Of Individuality</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society\">We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people\">Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people</a></li></ul><h5>IV. Of the Limits to the Authority of Society over the Individual</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases\">Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil\">One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil</a></li></ul><h5>V. Applications</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom\">One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom</a><ul><li>The limiting case: liberty cannot be used to alienate liberty.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child\">The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>There is some overlap between these two works, specifically in the way that they both treat the nature of people’s (moral) actions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Another work of liberal political theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a><ul><li>The Aristotelian account of equal friendship that <em>The Subjection of Women</em> arrives at from the opposite direction.</li></ul></li></ul>","One's fate is not determined by their birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s fate and course in life is not determine by the circumstances of their birth/growing up.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea reiterated in society and present in specific works of literature as well.</p><h4>East of Eden</h4><p>This is a main idea of East of Eden.</p><ul><li>It is first expressed during the naming ceremony by Sam Hamilton.</li><li>This is evident in the case of Adam, and Cal.</li></ul><p>There is large contrast between the way that Adam and Cyrus’s treatment of this idea, specifically regrading their respective offspring.</p><ul><li>Cyrus forces Adam into his own mold, giving him his own definition of a good life and morality, rather than giving him real moral choice.</li><li>While Adam fails in much the same way that Cyrus does throughout most of the book, in his final moment, he is able to redeem himself. His “timshel” blessing for Cal frees Cal from the moral determinism that has chased the members of the family throughout the rest of the book.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>The metaphysical version of the question. Freedom from one’s birth circumstances is compatible with one’s ultimate fate still being fixed.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our fate is determined from birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that our ultimate fates are ones that we do not get to determine. Rather, it is already determined, whether through from theological/divine process or a purely naturalistic one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a central idea in many faiths and belief system, and many works of art, so it is worth exploring.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>Oedipus Rex</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I would be blind to misery not to pity my people kneeling at my feet.</span><ul><li>This is a clever bit of irony that the play immediately throws at the audience, since at the end of the play, Oedipus is indeed blind, except he would be the one in misery, not his people.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">He said thieves attacked them—a whole band, not single-handed, cut King Laius down. OEDIPUS: A thief, so daring, so wild, he'd kill a king?</span><ul><li>Interesting how Oedipus mishears what Creon says and instead of referring to thieves, refers to a single, thief. It is as if he knows his fate, that he is that “thief” referred to.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Whoever killed the king may decide to kill me too, with the same violent hand—by avenging Laius I defend myself..</span><ul><li>First of all, this does not come true, at least in <em>Oedipus Rex</em>. Regardless, it is ironic that the “violent hand” that eventually kills Laius’ killer is Oedipus, the same person who states that this would occur.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Whoever he is, a lone man unknown in his crime or one among many, let that man drag out his life in agony, step by painful step—</span><ul><li>Oedipus not only makes reference to a single person to commits the crime, which is incongruous with the story told to him by Creon, he also predicts his future fate, where he is himself cast out of Thebes and forced to “drag out his life in agony, step by painful step–.”</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I curse myself as well . . . if by any chance he proves to be an intimate of our house, here at my hearth, with my full knowledge, may the curse I just called down on him strike me!</span><ul><li>This line perhaps hits too close to home for Oedipus, as he will soon learn.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I hold the throne that he held then, possess his bed and a wife who shares our seed . . . why, our seed might be the same, children born of the same mother might have created blood-bonds between us if his hope of offspring had not met disaster—</span><ul><li>Clearly, this is a prophecy Oedipus tells and which gets fulfilled by the end of the play.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">So ,I will fight for him as if he were my father</span><ul><li>Another bit of irony, as Laius is actually his <em>real</em> father.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Let no crops grow out of the earth for them— shrivel their women, kill their sons,</span><ul><li>This is indeed something that occurs in Antigone, as the sons of Oedipus kill each other, and Antigone herself is killed by Creon. Only Ismene survives.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Laius was killed, they say, by certain travelers. OEDIPUS: I know—but no one can find the murderer.</span><ul><li>Yet another instance of Oedipus (perhaps Freudian slipping) and stating that there was one murderer, instead of many.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Blind who now has eyes, beggar who now is rich, he will grope his way toward a foreign soil, a stick tapping before him step by step.</span><ul><li>Tiresias predicts Oedipus’ future, and states it to him.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">But reject a friend, a kinsman? I would as soon tear out the life within us, priceless life itself.</span><ul><li>This is exactly what Creon does in Antigone, as he kills Antigone. Therefore, it is rather ironic that he is moralizing Oedipus about this now.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Then let him go, even if it does lead to my ruin, my death or my disgrace, driven from Thebes for life.</span><ul><li>In a way, Creon is the one that eventually ruins Oedipus, since he did call Tiresias to meet with Oedipus, who reveals the nature of Oedipus’ relationship with his truth mother and father.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I heard all that and ran. I abandoned Corinth, from that day on I gauged its landfall only by the stars, running, always running toward some place where I would never see the shame of all those oracles come true.</span><ul><li>Oedipus attempts to escape his fate, and not see it come true. Yet, it is simply the case that he cannot see the truth, not that he is particularly able to escape the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">MESSENGER: Polybus was nothing to you, that's why, not in blood. OEDIPUS: What are you saying—Polybus was not my father? MESSENGER: No more than I am. He and I are equals.</span><ul><li>A chance meeting with a messenger (in fact, the very one that first brought the child Oedipus to Polybus) revealed the truth of Oedipus’ relationship with Polybus and Merope, showing the inevitability of fate.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">MESSENGER: He called himself a servant of . . . if I remember rightly—Laius. JOCASTA turns sharply.</span><ul><li>This seems to be the moment when Jocasta begins to piece things together, leading to her realization about Oedipus and her suicide.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">but I, I count myself the son of Chance,</span><ul><li>It is interesting how often Oedipus reference chance, here, he references himself as the child of chance. It seems that the very opposite is true, given the miraculous set of events that have occurred so far as to eventually lead Oedipus to find out the true nature of his birth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">My wife— she gave it to you?</span><ul><li>This is probably the deciding moment for Oedipus that made him actually realize the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Apollo, friends, Apollo— he ordained my agonies—these, my pains on pains! But the hand that struck my eyes was mine, mine alone—no one else— I did it all myself!</span><ul><li>It seems that although our fates are the determined, the way that we may react to our fates is not. This complicates the debate regarding free will, and how we are able to act in a world that is predetermined, yet still have moral consequences and be responsible for our own actions.</li><li>Clearly, Oedipus has to be responsible for his own actions (that of continuing to search for the truth), yet the truth itself has already been determined.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">It's mine alone, my destiny—I am Oedipus!</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Well let my destiny come and take me on its way!</span><ul><li>Oedipus accepts fate, at last (and with his eyes gone).</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Willie Stark</h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 89</p><p>Or it is possible that fellows like Willie Stark are born outside of luck, good or bad, and luck, which is what about makes you and me what we are, doesn’t have anything to do with them, <strong>for they are what they are from the time they first kick in the womb until the end</strong>. And if that is the case, then <strong>their life history is a process of discovering what they really are</strong>, and not, as for you and me, sons of luck, a process of becoming what luck makes us. And if that is the case, then Lucy wasn’t Willie’s luck. Or his unluck either. <strong>She was part of the climate in which the process of discovering the real Willie was taking place.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Willie Stark is said to essentially create his own luck (to the extent that the concept of “luck” doesn’t even meaningfully apply to him) since he goes through life with such brutal force and unmitigated determination.</li><li>All the other characters in the story are essentially part of the setting that revolves around Willie Stark.</li><li>It seems that the main motivation behind Stark’s unfiltered intensity is that his pursuits are, to him at least, almost divine callings. <ul><li>When Stark’s original parochial worldview had been shattered by Tiny Duffy’s offer, he gained almost a fanatical dedication to his life’s goal. </li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One&#x27;s fate is not determined by their birth\">One's fate is not determined by their birth</a><ul><li>Despite the near-identical titles, these are different claims. That note concerns social and familial circumstance; this one concerns metaphysical predetermination.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>It may well be the case that this endless pursuit of the truth is what ultimately determines our fate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>The fate that we are determined to, more often than not, could be one characterized by suffering (ex. Oedipus).</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The blind are able to see what the sighted are not\">The blind are able to see what the sighted are not</a><ul><li>Our fate is determined often because we are not able to see its warning signs, or we don’t even realize that our fate is determined. This could be because we are able to “see” in the normal sense, while to be able to “see” our fate requires a different sense.</li></ul></li></ul>","Randomization allows for causation to be determined":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Only when an experiment is randomized can we draw conclusions related to causation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant concept from statistics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Randomization reduces bias and confounding variables, allowing for causation to be determined.</p>","Returning unspent medical funds to the patient restores price sensitivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If a portion of the deductible is placed in an account that the patient may keep whatever they do not spend, then the patient bears the marginal cost of routine care and has a reason to compare prices, without losing protection against catastrophic expense.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the standard design for reintroducing a price-sensitive buyer into a third-party payment system, and it is the constructive half of the argument against pre-paid insurance.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The problem this design addresses.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation\">Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation</a><ul><li>What is lost when no participant faces the price, and what the account restores.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rituals are things that help us to maintain order in the presence of scarce resources (for instance, lining up in a queue helps us orderly manage our scarce resource of time), manage our emotions (as a funeral does when a death occurs), and helps us become human and form group bonds.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes some of the importance that rituals provide for humans.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Xunzi specifically argues that many of the rituals that are ostensibly without use (dancing a rain dance in order to bring about rain) actually serve a purpose, specifically as it is highlighted here.</p><h4>References</h4>","The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Luck Argument argues that indeterminism (i.e., that actions are not pre-determined) means that actions are the result of randomness. If that is the case, control over one’s actions is still nonexistent. Therefore, indeterminism does not actually support the case for Libertarianism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important critique of indeterminism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The incompatibilist premise the objection targets. Granting incompatibilism, libertarianism still does not follow.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism\">Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism</a><ul><li>A stronger conclusion in the same direction, since it denies free will whether or not compatibilism holds.</li></ul></li></ul>","The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the thesis that “good institutions” lead to economic growth is false because the concept of “good institutions” is fundamentally mistaken.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This rejects the thesis that there is such a thing as “good institutions,” or that it does not prove a causation that “good institutions lead to economic growth.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since it is the case that rich countries have many of the things that we associate with “good institutions,” it doesn’t mean that those things are necessarily the cause of the countries being rich. There is the risk of missing confounding factors and also the possibility of reverse causation. Overall, the concept of “institutions” is too vague. A better method of study is to look at specific institutions instead.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>The counterpart note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of predestination, which is a major aspect of Calvinism, provides a structure for the rationalization of moral life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Unlike the idea of “good works” present in other branches of Christianity, the idea of predestination entails the creation of a regimented life of asceticism (instead of just \"good works\"), as acting consistently with discipline represented the characteristics of someone who would go to heaven.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a></li></ul>","The permanent self does not exist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of a permanent self is fallacious, since every aspect of what can be considered the self is constantly in flux, and therefore, there cannot be a permanent, “true” self.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core concept in Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The Buddhist cannon identifies five aspects of experience: <em>Rūpa</em> (form), <em>Vedanā</em> (feeling), <em>Saṃjñā</em> (perception), <em>Saṃskāra</em> (mental formations), <em>Vijñāna</em> (consciousness). Each moment, each of the five elements change for a given person. Therefore, one cannot point to the existence of a self.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This view is weakened on a synchronic reading, since it seems like a bundle of experiences could define some sort of self in that way. However, a synchronic reading would have to solve the problem of defining what it is that ties the individual experiences together.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals\">Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals</a><ul><li>Another view on identity.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our identity is defined by our nature as agents\">Our identity is defined by our nature as agents</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self\">Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self</a><ul><li>Perhaps this view is related to the Buddhist view?</li></ul></li></ul>","There are several benefits to having big, dense cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many benefits that come from having big and dense cities, improving the productivity, increasing rates of innovation, and promoting equality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents an argument for why we should have big and dense cities.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ol><li>People are more productive in big cities. <ol><li>This can be seen through the fact that there is a noticeable wage premium when people come to work in big cities.</li><li>This effect is especially apparent when the city has an intangible economy, where there is a high concentration of intellectual value.</li></ol></li><li>Innovation increases when people congregate in big cities.<ol><li>Especially, there is an increase in innovation which required the combination of atypical combinations of knowledge.</li><li>Inventors who move from smaller clusters to bigger clusters tend to see large increases in their patenting productivity.</li></ol></li><li>By restricting housing, we are promoting inequality.<ol><li>Rising wages means that the poorer residents are priced out of their homes, and this leads to gentrification.</li><li>By allowing, for instance, local residents to vote on rezoning to increase density, we can solve this problem and ensure that the local residents capture the benefit of development instead of having that development displace them.</li><li>There is also regional inequality.<ol><li>Since it may be the case that for less well-paying jobs, it wouldn't be worth it for people to move to big cities where the rents are much higher compared to their wage increases, this means that people are just trapped in their original cities/states, increasing the disparity between states.</li></ol></li><li>High housing prices also reduce fertility.<ol><li>Women have less children than they say that they actually want. They often don't have children because it requires getting a bigger house, which they cannot afford.</li></ol></li></ol></li></ol><h4>Example</h4><p>One of the potential problems with African development is their lack of density. Their cities are currently not dense enough to produce the economic value generation that cities typically produce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>Dense cities also have dense rail networks, which also generate positive economic value.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention\">The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention</a><ul><li>Part of their success id due to the dense Japanese cities.</li></ul></li></ul>","To be courageous is not to be afraid of death":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that someone who is truly courageous is someone who is not afraid of death. Such a person will only fear “what is natural for men to fear” but that they will face such a fear in the “right way” and with principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since courage is a main moral characteristic that Aristotle analyzes.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Courage implies the presence of pain, and thus should be praised since it is more difficult to endure pain than abstain from pleasure.</p><h4>References</h4>","When a country hits its fiscal limit, it can suffer a range of negative consequences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a country hits its fiscal limit, it can suffer a range of negative consequences, including reduced market access, higher borrowing costs, currency depreciation, capital flight, or even economic contraction or default.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows why nations really do not want to hit the fiscal limit.</p><h4>Consequences</h4><p>The consequences for hitting the fiscal limit is high. It could be any of the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Reduced market access</strong>: investors buy less government debt or demand shorter term debt. It reduces the ability for the government to borrow/spend.</li><li><strong>Higher borrowing costs</strong>: markets demand greater interest on government debt since there is a higher perceived risk of default or inflation.</li><li><strong>Currency depreciation</strong>: the national currency is less demanded due to the high risk of government default. This raises the cost of foreign debt and imported goods.</li><li><strong>Capital flight</strong>: investors want to pull out their assets due to the high perceived risk of government default.</li></ul><p>All these could further contribute to economics contraction, or even government default.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation\">The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation</a></li></ul>","‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even beyond straight up despotism, the tyranny of the majority, manifested societally/culturally in modern times as “cancel culture,” is able to suppress individuality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows why tyranny of the majority is bad.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is bad on an individual level, since people’s unique individualities, something which is at least on some level a good in itself, is destroyed through censorship, which has the effect of making all the same. Also, this prevents the existence of geniuses who might think in unorthodox ways but end up hugely benefitting society.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This does not mean that we have to be entirely embracing of all people. Ultimately, we must also consider our own interest, though sometimes it may cause disutility for others. For instance, someone who is a compulsive liar might have few friends, though that no one wants to be friends with him is not a harm towards him (a suppression of their individuality), but merely a protection of our own interests.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>This is an elaboration of the concept from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
